A||||9||MooCow||Well I am not getting a Fiero||||2||
Z||000000||MooCow||04-27-2000||09:02 AM||||Well as of right now I am not going to be getting a Fiero. The reason is because I have done some thinking and since I am going to be a  poor college student in less than 2 months I need to have all the money I can get.  It seems to me that everyone that owns a Fiero does a lot of work on it and I do not have the time nor money to do that.  So I am most likly going to be getting a 93-97 extended cab sonaoma with a 4.3 auto for around $8,000-10,000 when I graduate.    I hope that in 5 years when I will have more money and more time I will get a Fiero cause my insurance company says that then it will be listed under a collectible and then really cheap insurance.  I will still be visiting here every day cause I love these little cars to death and one day I hope I will own one.<BR>Thanks for letting me ramble          Ken||||reg||2
Z||000001||lennyb4||04-27-2000||09:18 AM||||I would have to argue that statement with you. I have had two seperate fieros over the last 8 years. I never had to really do any work other then standard maint items. You need to remember that there are almost 1,000 seperate people asking questions in here and most of the problems are cheap and easy fixes. I can understand your point but i hope that one day you do get the chance to see just how fun these little cars are.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||04-27-2000||09:25 AM||||Personally I think the truck is a better call for a college student. I can't imagine packing the contents of a dorm room into a Fiero for summer break. A 2 seater is not the most practical mode of transportation for the transitory lifestyle of a student. Get yr degree and buy 2!  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||MooCow||04-27-2000||12:03 PM||||See I really want one but just don't want to  have to do to many repaires on it.  I may see what Ed Parks has for me with a 3.4.  That will make me have less problems with my engine.  Now its just the otehr things.<BR>As far as it goes for college I am living at home cause college is only like 3 miles away.  If I got a Fiero from Ed Parks I would have to drive the Fiero in winter but only for the first winter. Then I will hopefully have enough money to get a winter beast.  Thanks for all the posts so far. Ken||||reg||2
Z||000004||Phil||04-27-2000||05:57 PM||||I your school is only 3 miles away the worst thing you could do would be to drive any car such a short distance on a daily basis.  It will never get a chance to warm up and you will wind up putting 10 times the wear and tear on the motor.Get a bicycle or something. My $0.02||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||04-27-2000||09:28 PM||||I bought my 87 Fiero two years ago now, and the only work I have done to it is brakes, belts, and stereo.  I drive it about 4 times a week.  Reliablility is all in the car...for 8 grand you could have a sweet riding Fiero, but a Pickup is definitely more practical.<P>BTW, I only paid $1700 for mine and all it needed was some AC work.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jeff's '86 1/2 GT||04-27-2000||10:41 PM||||I have an '86 1/2 GT That my family bought brand new at the Pontiac Dealer.  I t has 285,000 miles on it.  I still have perfect oil pressure and don't burn ANY smoke in between 3000 mile oil changes.  The ONLY things that we have done to the car is: Brake pads, 2 clutches (last one a month ago), Heater core, Shocks & Springs, Clutch Master cylinder, and shifter cables (3 times now).  If that is alot of work to do to a car for you, then you just can't afford a car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  14 years and 285,000 miles later and I am STILL smiling as I pass cars!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shaft||04-28-2000||01:15 AM||||I would have to agree with MooCow and stimpy on this one. I have dreams of owning a fiero (esp. this black 88 that I drive by often which has two flats and hasn't moved in months) but I'm going into college and all my stuff wouldn't fit in a Fiero. Plus I'm goin to Mich. so the winters would kill me. All I can say for other people that want Fieros but need the extra luggage capacity is buy a rear clip and modify it into a trailer!||||reg||9
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||04-28-2000||03:20 AM||||I too want a Fiero (duh..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) but I also need some way to carry stuff. The solution? I want to get a Jeep Grand Cherokee as well.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>More expensive than a trailer.. but way cooler IMHO. and more practical, since I could drive that all year round, and not drive the fiero in winter.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DJRice||04-28-2000||09:16 AM||||Darklord, A Fiero and a Jeep Grand Cherokee make a good combination.  <P>I have an 87 SE V6, and a 96 G.C. Laredo.<BR>Its nice to be able to wake up one day, grab the keys to the Fiero, and feel the road right below your seat, and then the next day grab the keys to the jeep and see over everyone else on the road (with the exception of those Ford Excursions)<BR>||||reg||1
